WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.615
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.961 --> 00:00:10.189
&gt;&gt; I was hosting my first
dinner at Stanford.

00:00:10.189 --> 00:00:14.972
Already an insecure hostess, this is
was a pretty stressful occasion for me.

00:00:14.972 --> 00:00:18.990
I had managed spaghetti and
meatballs, which we'll call passable.

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:22.880
And I had purchased an obscene
quantity of cheap Trader Joe's wine

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.920
in an attempt to hide my poor cooking.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:29.480
We were well into our third bottle
when one of my new friends, Jennifer,

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:31.850
turned and said can I ask you a question?

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:33.000
Sure, I said.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.560
I hoped it wasn't going to be anything
about the cooking, because then I'd have

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.630
to admit that pretty much everything
in front of us had come out of a box.

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:45.160
Instead, she said Anna, you and
Jason have been married for two years.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.030
What do you think is the secret to love?

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:53.190
My heart was racing as the room turned and
stared at me.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:57.010
First, I definitely do not
believe that there is a secret.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:00.050
And secondly, I also did not
believe that being married for

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:03.435
two years qualified me as any
sort of relationship expert.

00:01:03.435 --> 00:01:07.930
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I can't remember what I said, but

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:12.547
I can guarantee that at that time
it was neither true nor helpful.

00:01:12.547 --> 00:01:17.000
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It was one of those moments where I

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.580
really, really wished I had an answer.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.700
I spent a lot of time
thinking about this question.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.670
And thanks to a lot of you who
came to me to ask love advice,

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:28.735
you know who you are, Jackie.

00:01:28.735 --> 00:01:35.100
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm here in front of you one and

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:39.670
a half years later with what I
think is a true and helpful answer.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.630
Love is a wave with highs and lows.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.650
Some of our deepest lows are conflict.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:53.340
And although our relationship might
appear perfect on the outside, Jason and

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:54.900
I are no different from most couples.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:00.110
One of our deepest lows
happened five years ago.

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:01.740
My mom was diagnosed with breast cancer.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:03.620
I was devastated.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:07.320
I called Jason, and
he came home from work immediately.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:11.660
He bought me a train ticket
from New York to Boston, and

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:14.420
he walked me to Penn Station
at four in the morning.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:19.340
The next few months were challenging,
to say the least.

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:23.550
I commuted from Boston to New York,
trying to care for my mother and

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:27.309
also trying to do a good job at my
work as an investment banking analyst.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.640
One weekend,
my mom seemed to be doing well, so

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:34.850
I decided that I was going to go home and
catch up on my work in New York.

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.500
Jason was on vacation across the country,
and

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.500
he decided to surprise
me by coming back early.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.070
Of course, I totally agreed to
go to a short dinner somewhere

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:44.620
in the neighborhood.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.380
30 minutes later,
we found ourselves still stuck in a cab.

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:55.630
Our cab driver had committed the 8th
cardinal sin known only to New Yorker's.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:58.901
He had taken us through Times Square.

00:02:58.901 --> 00:03:03.648
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; 45 minutes later we

00:03:03.648 --> 00:03:08.309
finally arrived at a charming
cubbyhole in the West Village,

00:03:08.309 --> 00:03:11.740
that charmingly,
kept its reservations on pen and paper.

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:14.300
And charmingly, had lost our reservation.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.860
An hour and
a half later we were finally seated.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:23.049
Two hours later we had obtained bread and
water with the promise of wine on the way.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:26.890
Two and a half hours later was
when I completely lost it.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:29.670
I got mad at Jason.

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:33.850
I yelled at him, and I accused him
of the most heinous of offenses,

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:36.050
trying to do something nice for me.

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:39.179
How dare he?

00:03:39.179 --> 00:03:42.862
Despite my egregious behavior,
we were married in 2012, and

00:03:42.862 --> 00:03:44.779
this is one of our wedding photos.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.820
Although I remembered
talking through this issue,

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.570
I still wasn't sure why Jason forgave me.

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:56.920
So a few weeks ago,
I called him to ask him about this and

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:02.050
he said, at the time,
you had an aura of gloom around you.

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:04.052
Seriously, an aura of gloom?

00:04:04.052 --> 00:04:07.760
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yes, an aura of gloom.

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:10.510
I blame the context.

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:11.290
I didn't blame you.

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:15.180
We had had so many happy memories, and

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:18.140
I could see a path forward to
many more happy memories to come.

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.429
Love is a wave with highs and lows.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:28.130
When I was researching love I came across
the work of Chipney, a psychologist who

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:32.610
studies how we behave in relationships,
particularly during conflict.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:36.710
As it turns out, we all have these mental
models that deeply impact how we behave,

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:37.940
even though we don't realize.

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:43.730
The first is this concept of destiny,
the idea of meant to be.

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:45.640
We're all familiar with this concept,

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:49.590
the thought that there is one person
out there who we're meant of find.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:50.920
I blame Hollywood and Disney.

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:55.550
The second is the idea of growth.

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:57.740
This is based on Carol Dweck's
work on mindset.

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:03.740
Carol says that people who have a growth
mindset tend to embrace failure.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:05.620
They view it as an opportunity to learn.

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:10.410
People with a fixed mindset instead
view failure as a ding against their

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:12.900
intelligence and they stop trying.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:16.460
It turns out that it's remarkably
similar in relationships.

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:21.380
People who are high on the growth spectrum
tend to view conflict as inevitable,

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:23.540
as something that needs
to be worked through.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:26.090
And even as an opportunity to
become closer to your partner.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:30.799
People who are low on
the spectrum give up very easily.

00:05:32.216 --> 00:05:33.740
Destiny is much more complicated.

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:38.140
Early on,
it can completely kill a relationship.

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:41.680
After all, you wouldn't be having a petty
squabble with your loved one about where

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:44.310
to go for dinner on Friday night if
you were meant to be with them, right?

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:49.260
Later on, it's much more complicated.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:52.050
On the one hand, it can make
you work harder to salvage this

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:53.660
relationship that you're meant to be in.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.930
But it can also cause denial and
avoidance of problems.

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:04.520
What's really powerful to me about all of
this is it is completely in our minds.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:05.850
We can control it.

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:09.982
If we choose to put aside
this concept of destiny and

00:06:09.982 --> 00:06:14.635
instead embrace the idea of growth, and
the idea of working through the lows,

00:06:14.635 --> 00:06:17.895
we can all have happier and
healthier relationships.

00:06:20.658 --> 00:06:22.619
When I thought of couples
who did this well,

00:06:22.619 --> 00:06:26.080
I immediately thought of
my husband's grandparents.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:29.630
Grandma Ada and
Pops have been married for 67 years.

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:35.110
Two years ago we celebrated
their 65th anniversary.

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:38.560
It was so moving to see how they
happy they were to have their kids,

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:42.700
their grandkids, and their great-grandkids
all assembled in one room.

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:48.495
This is a photo of them in
1949 when they got married.

00:06:48.495 --> 00:06:52.652
And this is another photo more
recently taken right before the party.

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:57.227
When I called to ask them what
they thought about love and

00:06:57.227 --> 00:06:59.970
relationships, I learned
some interesting things.

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:06.150
First, I learned that my husband
had sneakily been talking to Pops

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:08.825
almost since the day we got married and
getting his advice.

00:07:08.825 --> 00:07:13.217
I would argue that Jason has been
employing Pops' advice to great effect in

00:07:13.217 --> 00:07:15.340
our relationship.

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:19.750
According to Pops, there are three little
words that can make a successful marriage.

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:22.818
And they are yes, my dear.

00:07:22.818 --> 00:07:30.135
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:07:30.135 --> 00:07:33.680
in all seriousness, Gramma Ada did give
me some real material to work with.

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:38.710
She said we've shared
some incredible highs,

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:41.450
the birth of our children as one example.

00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:46.000
We've also shared some really deep lows,
in particular the health problems they've

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:48.430
experienced recently as
they've gotten older.

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:54.580
At any moment, she said, you have
the choice to stay in a relationship or

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:57.570
to leave it,
particularly when times get tough.

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:02.600
Maybe I'm a little bit higher on
destiny than I'd like to admit, but

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:03.170
I was surprised.

00:08:03.170 --> 00:08:07.290
I thought that they were going to
talk about love and the meant to be.

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:10.830
And instead, both of them just
talked about how hard it was.

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:12.710
This wasn't at all what
I was expecting to hear.

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:19.913
Love is a wave with highs and lows.

00:08:19.913 --> 00:08:24.503
My wish for all of us sitting in this
room today is that we can put aside this

00:08:24.503 --> 00:08:28.718
idea of destiny, that we can stop
thinking about the meant to be and

00:08:28.718 --> 00:08:31.076
instead embrace what we have today.

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:36.632
If we embrace the work that
the lows take to get through,

00:08:36.632 --> 00:08:42.489
we can all have healthier and
happier friendships, family relationships,

00:08:42.489 --> 00:08:47.106
and yes, even romantic
relationships with our loved ones.

00:08:47.106 --> 00:08:48.337
Thank you.

00:08:48.337 --> 00:08:50.960
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:55.139
[MUSIC]

